# Task: CodeGenome Connectors (Git, Azure DevOps Boards, Jira)
---

## 1. Why we are building this

CodeGenome today needs a **.zip upload** to start a discovery run, and it has no link to the client's work tracking. Connectors fix both:

1. **Source intake** – start a discovery run from a Git repository and commit instead of a zip.
2. **Work items in** – pull defects/incidents from Jira or Azure DevOps Boards so the platform knows the support history of each component.
3. **Work items out** – push the modernization plan (waves, modules, acceptance criteria from recovered business rules) into Jira or ADO Boards.
4. **Agent access** – let agents read tickets and propose updates through MCP, with a human approval before any write.

## 2. Scope

**In scope**

- Git source connector: GitHub, Azure DevOps Repos, GitLab (GitHub + ADO Repos first)
- Work-item connectors: Azure DevOps Boards, Jira Cloud
- Webhooks + polling fallback, idempotent push, health/freshness, audit log
- Vendor MCP servers configured for agents (read-only by default)
- A small CodeGenome "work-items" MCP facade (3 tools)

## 3. Architecture

```
Agents (Claude Agent SDK / OpenCode / Kiro)
   │ MCP
   ├── Vendor MCP servers (Atlassian, Azure DevOps, GitHub)   ← read-only, allowlisted per tenant
   └── codegenome-workitems MCP (our facade, 3 tools)          ← M7
            │
   Connector sync service (Python, REST/SDK)                    ← M1–M5: you build this
     ├── webhooks  (POST /webhooks/{kind}/{connector_id})
     ├── polling   (scheduled pull_changes since last cursor)
     ├── push      (idempotent upsert, dry-run by default)
     └── health    (last sync, errors, backlog)
            │
   Git · Azure DevOps Boards · Jira Cloud
```

**Rule of thumb:** anything scheduled, event-driven, bulk or idempotent goes in the **sync service (plain REST)**. Anything an agent decides to do at runtime goes through **MCP**.

